asdf itself is installed by ../development/Brewfile, without any plugins.
Which tools and versions are needed is up to each project instead.

Projects say which versions they need either in an asdf .tool-versions
file (see ./tool-versions.example), or in the version files other tools
use: .nvmrc / .node-version (Node) and .java-version / .sdkmanrc (Java).
asdf reads those too thanks to `legacy_version_file = yes` in
./.asdfrc.symlink, which `dotfiles bootstrap` links to ~/.asdfrc.

In a project's directory, run:

    asdf-sync

This adds any missing asdf plugins and installs the versions the project
asks for. ./path.zsh puts this folder on the PATH, so the command is
available in every shell. Note it only looks in the current directory,
not in parent directories the way asdf itself does. From .sdkmanrc only
the Java version is used (repos usually ship mvnw/gradlew for the rest).

To set up global defaults in ~/.tool-versions from a file kept outside
this repo (e.g. the tool-versions of a work machine):

    dotfiles install asdf ~/dev/tool-versions

Leave the file argument out to be asked for it (defaults to
~/dev/tool-versions).
